//@version=6
// ============================================================================
// Edge Atlas [WavesUnchained]
// Version: 1.7.0
// Author: WavesUnchained
// Build: 2026-08-11
// ============================================================================
// Right-edge price-level atlas.
//
// Core idea:
//   • Calculation modules produce normalized price levels.
//   • A shared registry filters and renders them.
//   • Each level renders as a label a fixed bar count past the last real
//     candle — pointer facing right, price/zone detail in the hover tooltip
//     only — with a short, fixed-length rail picking up just past the label.
//     The rail does not reach the chart's true right edge; keeping the
//     current price area clear of labels matters more than the rail
//     visually touching the price scale.
//   • Nothing about the label/rail's position depends on the current
//     viewport (no chart.right_visible_bar_time/left_visible_bar_time reads,
//     no xloc.bar_time) — panning or zooming the chart moves them exactly
//     like any normal drawing, with nothing to jump or freeze mid-drag.
//   • Line width and label/rail opacity scale with each level's priority —
//     high-priority levels (PDH/PDL-class) render bolder and more opaque,
//     low-priority ones fainter — the same "visual weight tracks
//     significance" principle Fib Reaction Memory uses for its Memory Score.
//
// Features:
//   ✓ Previous/current day, week, month, and year reference levels
//   ✓ Traditional and Fibonacci pivot points
//   ✓ Swing-based Fibonacci retracements/extensions
//   ✓ Pivot-cluster support/resistance zones
//   ✓ Asia, London, and New York session ranges
//   ✓ Automatic or manual round-number levels
//   ✓ Session, weekly, and monthly VWAP
//   ✓ Up to 4 manually entered price levels
//   ✓ Two freely nameable custom sessions, in addition to Asia/London/NY
//   ✓ Traditional, Fibonacci, Camarilla, Woodie, and DeMark pivot methods
//   ✓ Trend Curves optionally feed the right-edge Level registry too, and
//     can compute on a higher/lower timeframe than the chart's own
//   ✓ Daily, weekly, and monthly opening ranges
//   ✓ Granular sub-level switches and explanatory tooltips
//   ✓ Distance, priority, count, and side filters
//   ✓ Minimum label spacing scaled to the instrument's own ATR, so crowded
//     levels don't collapse into a solid overlapping block, and which levels
//     are shown stays stable while scrolling or zooming the chart
//   ✓ Line width and opacity scale with priority, so the most significant
//     levels stand out from the rest at a glance
//   ✓ Optional source zones in the chart
//   ✓ Trend Curves — up to three JMA-style/EMA/SMA moving averages with
//     Responsive/Balanced/Structural/Custom presets, plotted as familiar,
//     always-on chart lines (separate from the right-edge level registry)
//
// Important TradingView limitation:
// Pine drawings can bind to chart time and price, but not to arbitrary screen
// pixels or the price scale itself. The label anchor is a simple forward
// offset — last real bar's bar_index plus a fixed bar count ("Label offset ·
// bars") — deliberately NOT a percentage of the visible bar range, and NOT
// computed by subtracting from chart.right_visible_bar_time either. Both of
// those were tried:
//   • A percentage of the visible range recomputes on every scroll/zoom.
//     Since Pine only redraws when the script recalculates (not continuously
//     during a drag), the label would freeze mid-pan and then visibly jump
//     to a new position whenever the next recalculation happened, instead of
//     panning smoothly like a normal drawing.
//   • Subtracting from chart.right_visible_bar_time is even less stable: that
//     live edge value can itself shift because of this script's own
//     future-projected drawings, which made labels behave unpredictably
//     (ending up right back at the last candle instead of near the edge).
// A forward offset from bar_index (which only changes when new bars close,
// never on chart navigation) keeps the label at a fixed position — redraws
// land exactly where it already was, so there is nothing to jump. This is
// the same positioning principle Fib Reaction Memory uses for its projected
// Fib lines (`bar_index + rightProjection`, xloc.bar_index) — this script
// used an equivalent xloc.bar_time offset from `time` through v1.6.0; both
// forms are fixed, viewport-independent offsets, but bar_index is simpler
// and needs no per-bar duration bookkeeping.
//
// Labels use label.style_label_right — the pointer sits at that anchor,
// facing right, and the box extends back toward price from there. That
// growth direction is inherently riskier than growing away from price (an
// earlier version deliberately avoided it after repeated overlap bugs), so
// two things keep it safe here: the label body is short (code/name only, no
// price — full detail is in the hover tooltip), and the offset defaults
// generously (180 bars, capped at 300). If labels ever start touching
// candles again on an extremely zoomed-out chart, increase "Label offset ·
// bars" first.
//
// Two independent bugs produced the same visual symptom — a vertical
// multi-colored bar through the label stack — while this was being tuned,
// and both are relevant if it ever reappears:
//   1. Every level's rail shared the exact same starting x-coordinate
//      (computed once outside the per-level loop). Several differently-
//      colored horizontal lines beginning at the identical pixel column,
//      stacked closely in price, read as a vertical bar regardless of
//      segment length — fixed by staggering each rail's start by render
//      rank, so no two levels can ever share an origin.
//   2. Pine only allows drawings a bounded number of bars into the future
//      (roughly 500). A percentage of a wide 15m/1h visible history could
//      exceed that, silently clamping every level to the same maximum
//      allowed point regardless of the stagger above — fixed by capping
//      every rail endpoint well under that ceiling.
// The rail no longer uses extend.right to reach the true edge (dropped on
// request), which incidentally removes most of the exposure to bug #2 for
// the rail itself — but the label's own anchor is still a raw future
// bar_index, so the cap remains necessary regardless.
// ============================================================================

indicator(
     "Edge Atlas [WavesUnchained]",
     shorttitle = "Edge Atlas",
     overlay = true,
     max_lines_count = 500,
     max_labels_count = 500,
     max_boxes_count = 100
)

// ============================================================================
// Constants
// ============================================================================

string G_GENERAL  = "00 — General"
string G_RENDER   = "01 — Right-edge rendering"
string G_DAY      = "02 — Period levels · Day"
string G_WEEK     = "03 — Period levels · Week"
string G_MONTH    = "04 — Period levels · Month"
string G_YEAR     = "05 — Period levels · Year"
string G_SESSIONS = "06 — Sessions"
string G_PIVOTS   = "07 — Pivot points"
string G_FIB      = "08 — Swing Fibonacci"
string G_SR       = "09 — Support / Resistance"
string G_ROUND    = "10 — Round numbers"
string G_VWAP     = "11 — VWAP"
string G_MANUAL   = "12 — Manual levels"
string G_OR       = "13 — Opening ranges"
string G_TREND    = "14 — Trend Curves"

string TT_EDGE =
     "How many bars past the last real candle the label sits — higher values keep the current price area clearer of labels.\n" +
     "A fixed bar count rather than a percentage of the visible time range on purpose: a percentage-based offset changes every time the visible range changes (any scroll or zoom), which made the label freeze mid-drag and then jump to a new position on the next recalculation instead of following the pan smoothly. A fixed bar count stays at the same calendar time regardless of scroll position, so it pans with the chart like a normal drawing.\n" +
     "On a chart showing a lot of history (many bars in view), a fixed bar count is a smaller fraction of the visible width, so it can still end up looking tight against the last candle — increase it if that happens; there is no single value that looks the same at every zoom level.\n" +
     "Limited to 400 bars here, well clear of Pine's bounded future-drawing distance (roughly 500 bars) — the rails for the highest-priority rendered levels are separately clamped even closer to that limit, so raising \"Maximum rendered levels\" a lot at the same time as this can't push anything past it."

string TT_DISTANCE =
     "Only levels within this ATR distance from the current close are eligible.\n" +
     "Set a high value to deliberately allow a dense or overloaded display."

string TT_MISSING =
     "A level is not registered when its source value is na.\n" +
     "Session ranges therefore remain absent when no chart bars exist inside the configured window."

string TT_TREND_PRESET =
     "Responsive (JMA-style 9 / 21 / 50): intraday and short-term swing trading, fast markets (oil, indices, forex, crypto) — early pullback and trend-change detection. Three fast adaptive lines react alike but can still whipsaw in choppy sideways phases.\n" +
     "Balanced (JMA-style 21 / EMA 50 / SMA 200): classic day/swing trading, stocks/indices/commodities — a movement line, a tradable-trend line, and a slow regime anchor. Default.\n" +
     "Structural (EMA 20 / SMA 50 / SMA 200): swing/position trading, daily/weekly charts — filters short-term noise, reads broader trend structure and regime shifts (e.g. the 50/200 cross).\n" +
     "Custom: set type, length, phase, and power per line below. Lengths are always in bars of the current chart timeframe and are not auto-adjusted — 20 always means 20 candles, whatever the timeframe."

string TT_JMA =
     "JMA-style: an open-source, Jurik-inspired adaptive smoothing approximation (tunable phase/power) — not Mark Jurik's patented, proprietary original algorithm."

// ============================================================================
// Inputs · General
// ============================================================================

string presetInput = input.string(
     "Clean",
     "Preset",
     options = ["Clean", "Intraday", "Structure", "Everything", "Custom"],
     tooltip =
         "Clean: period references + nearest S/R.\n" +
         "Intraday: adds session ranges, daily pivots, and Session VWAP.\n" +
         "Structure: adds swing Fibonacci and S/R.\n" +
         "Everything: activates every calculation module.\n" +
         "Custom: uses only the individual module switches below.",
     group = G_GENERAL
)

bool masterEnableInput = input.bool(true, "Enable indicator", group = G_GENERAL)
int maxLevelsInput = input.int(
     24, "Maximum rendered levels",
     minval = 1, maxval = 120,
     tooltip = "Hard cap after all active modules have registered their levels.",
     group = G_GENERAL
)
float maxDistanceAtrInput = input.float(
     12.0, "Maximum distance from price · ATR",
     minval = 0.25, maxval = 500.0, step = 0.25,
     tooltip = TT_DISTANCE,
     group = G_GENERAL
)
bool showAboveInput = input.bool(true, "Levels above price", inline = "sides", group = G_GENERAL)
bool showBelowInput = input.bool(true, "Levels below price", inline = "sides", group = G_GENERAL)
int minPriorityInput = input.int(
     1, "Minimum priority",
     minval = 1, maxval = 10,
     tooltip = "1 keeps everything. Higher values remove less important levels first.",
     group = G_GENERAL
)

// ============================================================================
// Inputs · Rendering
// ============================================================================

int labelOffsetBarsInput = input.int(
     200, "Label offset · bars",
     minval = 5, maxval = 400, step = 5,
     tooltip = TT_EDGE,
     group = G_RENDER
)
float minLabelSpacingAtrInput = input.float(
     3.0, "Minimum label spacing · ATR",
     minval = 0.0, maxval = 20.0, step = 0.1,
     tooltip =
         "Levels closer than this ATR distance to an already-selected, higher-priority level are skipped.\n" +
         "Tied to the instrument's own volatility rather than the current scroll position, so which levels are shown stays stable while panning the chart — an earlier percentage-of-visible-range version changed the level selection as you scrolled, since the visible price range itself changed.\n" +
         "ATR is an imperfect proxy for the label's actual pixel height: a low-volatility instrument/timeframe combination can still overlap even at a fairly high multiple. If labels still overlap with several modules enabled at once, increase this first; \"Maximum rendered levels\" and \"Maximum distance from price · ATR\" (both in Right-edge rendering) are the other two levers if a preset simply has too many modules active for the available vertical space.\n" +
         "Without this, nearby levels stack into overlapping label boxes that read as a solid colored block.\n" +
         "Set to 0 to disable and show every eligible level regardless of crowding.",
     group = G_RENDER
)
string labelContentInput = input.string(
     "Code",
     "Label content",
     options = ["Code", "Full name"],
     tooltip = "Price, zone bounds, and touch count are never shown in the label body — they're always in the hover tooltip, keeping the label itself short.",
     group = G_RENDER
)
string labelSizeInput = input.string(
     "Small",
     "Label size",
     options = ["Tiny", "Small", "Normal"],
     group = G_RENDER
)
int lineWidthInput = input.int(2, "Line width", minval = 1, maxval = 4, group = G_RENDER)
int lineTransparencyInput = input.int(
     10, "Line transparency",
     minval = 0, maxval = 95,
     group = G_RENDER
)
int labelTransparencyInput = input.int(
     10, "Label transparency",
     minval = 0, maxval = 95,
     group = G_RENDER
)
bool showCategoryInput = input.bool(
     false, "Prefix category",
     tooltip = "Example: PERIOD · PDH 24,500.00",
     group = G_RENDER
)
bool showSourceZonesInput = input.bool(
     false, "Show S/R source zones in chart",
     tooltip = "Draws the selected support/resistance zones backward through the chart.",
     group = G_RENDER
)

// ============================================================================
// Inputs · Period levels
// ============================================================================

bool periodModuleInput = input.bool(
     true, "Enable day levels",
     tooltip = "Master switch for daily period references. Only takes effect on the Custom preset — the other presets decide this module on/off themselves.",
     group = G_DAY, active = presetInput == "Custom"
)
bool showPDHInput = input.bool(
     true, "PDH",
     tooltip = "PDH — Previous Day High\nHighest price of the previous daily period.\nOften watched as intraday resistance/liquidity — a clean break can extend a rally, a rejection can mark the day's high.",
     inline = "d1", group = G_DAY, active = presetInput == "Custom" and periodModuleInput
)
bool showPDLInput = input.bool(
     true, "PDL",
     tooltip = "PDL — Previous Day Low\nLowest price of the previous daily period.\nOften watched as intraday support/liquidity — a clean break can extend a decline, a rejection can mark the day's low.",
     inline = "d1", group = G_DAY, active = presetInput == "Custom" and periodModuleInput
)
bool showPDCInput = input.bool(
     true, "PDC",
     tooltip = "PDC — Previous Day Close\nClosing price of the previous daily period.\nReference for gap analysis (today's open above/below PDC = gap up/down) and a common mean-reversion magnet.",
     inline = "d1", group = G_DAY, active = presetInput == "Custom" and periodModuleInput
)
bool showPDOInput = input.bool(
     false, "PDO",
     tooltip = "PDO — Previous Day Open\nOpening price of the previous daily period.\nMarks where the previous day's own trend began — useful for judging how far that day's move ran.",
     inline = "d2", group = G_DAY, active = presetInput == "Custom" and periodModuleInput
)
bool showDOInput = input.bool(
     true, "DO",
     tooltip = "DO — Current Day Open\nOpening price of the current daily period.\nMany intraday systems key bias off price's position relative to DO (above = long-favored, below = short-favored).",
     inline = "d2", group = G_DAY, active = presetInput == "Custom" and periodModuleInput
)
bool showPDMInput = input.bool(
     false, "PDM",
     tooltip = "PDM — Previous Day Midpoint\nArithmetic midpoint of previous day high and low.\nRough fair-value reference for the prior day — trading in the upper/lower half frames intraday strength/weakness.",
     inline = "d2", group = G_DAY, active = presetInput == "Custom" and periodModuleInput
)

bool weekModuleInput = input.bool(
     true, "Enable week levels",
     tooltip = "Master switch for weekly period references. Only takes effect on the Custom preset.",
     group = G_WEEK, active = presetInput == "Custom"
)
bool showPWHInput = input.bool(
     true, "PWH",
     tooltip = "PWH — Previous Week High.\nSwing resistance/liquidity — stops often cluster just above it, so a sweep followed by a reclaim is a common reversal pattern here.",
     inline = "w1", group = G_WEEK, active = presetInput == "Custom" and weekModuleInput
)
bool showPWLInput = input.bool(
     true, "PWL",
     tooltip = "PWL — Previous Week Low.\nSwing support/liquidity — stops often cluster just below it, so a sweep followed by a reclaim is a common reversal pattern here.",
     inline = "w1", group = G_WEEK, active = presetInput == "Custom" and weekModuleInput
)
bool showPWCInput = input.bool(
     false, "PWC",
     tooltip = "PWC — Previous Week Close.\nReference for weekly gap analysis and a slower-moving mean-reversion magnet than PDC.",
     inline = "w1", group = G_WEEK, active = presetInput == "Custom" and weekModuleInput
)
bool showPWOInput = input.bool(
     false, "PWO",
     tooltip = "PWO — Previous Week Open.\nMarks where the previous week's own trend began — useful for judging how far that week's move ran.",
     inline = "w2", group = G_WEEK, active = presetInput == "Custom" and weekModuleInput
)
bool showWOInput = input.bool(
     true, "WO",
     tooltip = "WO — Current Week Open.\nCommon weekly bias reference — many swing traders read above/below WO as the week's directional lean.",
     inline = "w2", group = G_WEEK, active = presetInput == "Custom" and weekModuleInput
)
bool showPWMInput = input.bool(
     false, "PWM",
     tooltip = "PWM — Previous Week Midpoint.\nFair-value reference for the prior week — trading in the upper/lower half frames swing strength/weakness.",
     inline = "w2", group = G_WEEK, active = presetInput == "Custom" and weekModuleInput
)

bool monthModuleInput = input.bool(
     true, "Enable month levels",
     tooltip = "Master switch for monthly period references. Only takes effect on the Custom preset.",
     group = G_MONTH, active = presetInput == "Custom"
)
bool showPMHInput = input.bool(
     false, "PMH",
     tooltip = "PMH — Previous Month High.\nHigher-timeframe structure — a major liquidity/resistance level that tends to matter across many lower timeframes at once.",
     inline = "m1", group = G_MONTH, active = presetInput == "Custom" and monthModuleInput
)
bool showPMLInput = input.bool(
     false, "PML",
     tooltip = "PML — Previous Month Low.\nHigher-timeframe structure — a major liquidity/support level that tends to matter across many lower timeframes at once.",
     inline = "m1", group = G_MONTH, active = presetInput == "Custom" and monthModuleInput
)
bool showPMCInput = input.bool(
     false, "PMC",
     tooltip = "PMC — Previous Month Close.\nReference for monthly gap analysis and a slow, higher-timeframe mean-reversion magnet.",
     inline = "m1", group = G_MONTH, active = presetInput == "Custom" and monthModuleInput
)
bool showPMOInput = input.bool(
     false, "PMO",
     tooltip = "PMO — Previous Month Open.\nMarks where the previous month's own trend began — useful for judging how far that month's move ran.",
     inline = "m2", group = G_MONTH, active = presetInput == "Custom" and monthModuleInput
)
bool showMOInput = input.bool(
     true, "MO",
     tooltip = "MO — Current Month Open.\nCommon macro bias reference — many position traders read above/below MO as the month's directional lean.",
     inline = "m2", group = G_MONTH, active = presetInput == "Custom" and monthModuleInput
)
bool showPMMInput = input.bool(
     false, "PMM",
     tooltip = "PMM — Previous Month Midpoint.\nFair-value reference for the prior month — trading in the upper/lower half frames longer-term strength/weakness.",
     inline = "m2", group = G_MONTH, active = presetInput == "Custom" and monthModuleInput
)

